    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Solar vs wired security cameras: the short answer</h2>
<p>A solar security camera is a battery camera recharged by a small panel. It connects over Wi-Fi or 4G and records short clips when motion wakes it. A wired security camera gets power from a cable, either a plug-in adapter or, most commonly for serious systems, a single Ethernet cable carrying both power and data (Power over Ethernet, or PoE). It can run and record all the time.</p>
<p>That one difference, event-based battery power versus continuous mains power, drives almost every other trade-off. Solar is cheaper and faster to install in awkward places. Wired is more capable and more predictable once installed. Neither is universally better; the right answer depends on where the camera goes and what you need it to do.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/placement-options.webp" alt="Different camera placement options around a house, garage and outbuildings" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Where the camera goes often decides solar vs wired: near power, or far from it.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Solar camera vs wired security camera: full comparison</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Solar (battery + panel)</th><th>Wired (PoE or plug-in)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Installation</td><td>DIY in about 30–60 min per camera; no cables to the house</td><td>Cable run per camera; may need drilling through walls, attic work or trenching</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upfront cost</td><td>About $60–$300 per camera, plus panel if not included</td><td>About $250–$1,200 for a 4–8 camera PoE NVR kit, plus labor if needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Running cost</td><td>Optional cloud plan; 4G data plan for cellular models</td><td>Small electricity cost; optional cloud</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recording mode</td><td>Motion events, short clips</td><td>Continuous 24/7 plus event tagging</td></tr>
<tr><td>Video quality</td><td>2K–4K, but lower bitrate and frame rate to save power</td><td>2K–4K+ at full bitrate and frame rate all the time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Response time</td><td>Short wake-up delay; may miss the first moment</td><td>Always recording; nothing missed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Depends on sun, battery health and wireless signal</td><td>Very consistent; depends on cable and NVR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power outages</td><td>Unaffected (camera); Wi-Fi router may go down</td><td>Down unless NVR and PoE switch are on a UPS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Clean panel, watch battery, replace battery every 2–4+ years</td><td>Very little; occasional firmware and disk checks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather</td><td>Winter and shade reduce charging; cold can stop charging</td><td>Unaffected by sun; cold rarely matters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Theft and tampering</td><td>Easier to remove; footage lost if stored only on SD</td><td>Footage stored safely indoors on NVR</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scalability</td><td>Easy to add one at a time; app juggling at scale</td><td>Centralized; cheaper per camera beyond about 6 cameras</td></tr>
<tr><td>Portability</td><td>Move it in minutes; renter-friendly</td><td>Fixed installation</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Are wired cameras better than solar?</h2>
<p>For pure performance, yes. A wired PoE camera is always on, records every second, streams at full quality and doesn't care if it has been cloudy for two weeks. It also keeps footage indoors on an NVR where a thief can't easily reach it. For a business, a high-traffic entrance or anywhere you need complete footage for insurance, wired is the stronger choice.</p>
<p>But "better" depends on the spot. A wired camera is only better if you can actually get a cable to it at a reasonable cost. For a gate 400 feet down a driveway, a barn across a field or a rental where you can't drill, the cable run is the problem, and a solar camera that covers the spot beats a wired camera that never gets installed.</p>

<h3>Where solar wins</h3>
<div class="proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Solar pros</h4><ul><li>No cables, trenching or electrician</li><li>Installs almost anywhere with sun</li><li>Keeps working through power outages</li><li>Portable and renter-friendly</li><li>Lower upfront cost for 1–4 cameras in hard-to-wire spots</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Solar cons</h4><ul><li>Event-based recording, not 24/7</li><li>Sensitive to shade, winter and signal</li><li>Battery wears out over years</li><li>Easier to steal</li><li>Short wake-up delay on each event</li></ul></div></div>

<h3>Where wired wins</h3>
<div class="proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Wired pros</h4><ul><li>Continuous 24/7 recording</li><li>Consistent full-quality video</li><li>No batteries or panels to maintain</li><li>Footage stored safely on an indoor NVR</li><li>Lowest cost per camera at scale</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Wired cons</h4><ul><li>Cable installation can be costly or impossible</li><li>Fixed placement</li><li>Goes down in power cuts without a UPS</li><li>Ethernet runs limited to about 100 m (328 ft) without extenders</li></ul></div></div>

<h2>PoE vs solar: how the power actually works</h2>
<p>PoE sends DC power and data down a single Cat5e or Cat6 cable from a PoE switch or a PoE NVR. Standard PoE (IEEE 802.3af) delivers up to about 15 W per port, and PoE+ (802.3at) up to about 30 W, more than enough for a camera with IR, heaters or a pan-tilt motor. Because power is effectively unlimited, a PoE camera never has to sleep. The trade-off is the cable: each camera needs its own run back to the switch, with a practical limit of about 100 m (328 ft). The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_over_Ethernet" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Power over Ethernet</a> standards define these limits.</p>
<p>A solar camera has a tight energy budget instead. A small 3–6 W panel might deliver 5–15 Wh on a good day, and the camera stores it in an 18–37 Wh battery. That's enough for a camera that sleeps most of the time and wakes on motion, but not for one that records continuously. Our guide to <a href="/how-solar-cameras-work/">how solar cameras work</a> walks through the numbers, and the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-battery-calculator/">solar camera battery calculator</a> lets you check a specific setup.</p>
<div class="keyfacts"><div><b>~15–30 W</b><span>power available per PoE / PoE+ port</span></div><div><b>5–15 Wh</b><span>daily output of a small camera panel</span></div><div><b>100 m</b><span>max Ethernet run without extenders</span></div></div>

<h2>Installation compared: what each one actually involves</h2>
<p>Installation is where the two options differ most in day-to-day terms, and it is usually what tips the decision.</p>
<h3>Installing a solar camera</h3>
<p>A solar camera install is a one-person job for most homeowners. You charge the battery, pair the camera in the app, check the Wi-Fi or 4G signal at the spot, then screw a bracket to the wall and a second bracket for the panel where it gets direct sun. The only cable is the short lead from the panel to the camera. Expect about 30–60 minutes per camera. The hard part is choosing a spot with both a good view and enough sun; the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-site-check/">solar camera site check</a> helps with that, and our <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">solar CCTV installation guide</a> covers each step.</p>
<h3>Installing a wired PoE camera</h3>
<p>A wired install starts with the NVR or PoE switch somewhere indoors, usually near the router. From there, each camera needs its own Ethernet cable. On a house with an accessible attic, that typically means drilling through the soffit, pulling cable across the attic and down to the NVR location, then crimping or plugging the ends. Expect a few hours for the first camera and less for each one after. Through finished walls, brick or between floors, the job gets harder, and many people hire a low-voltage installer.</p>
<p>For outbuildings, the cable has to cross open ground. Outdoor direct-burial Ethernet in a trench is the usual approach, and runs over about 100 m need a switch or fiber link partway. That trenching is the single biggest cost difference between the two options.</p>
<h3>Plug-in wired cameras</h3>
<p>A middle option is a plug-in Wi-Fi camera that takes power from an outdoor outlet. It records continuously like a wired camera but sends video over Wi-Fi. It suits spots with an existing outlet nearby. If there is no outlet, adding one usually needs an electrician, which can cost as much as a solar camera.</p>

<h2>Solar vs wired security cameras: cost comparison</h2>
<p>Hardware is only part of the cost. For wired cameras, the cable run often costs more than the cameras themselves. For solar, ongoing subscriptions and battery replacement add up over time. The ranges below are typical US retail and labor at time of writing (late 2026) and vary widely by region and site.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost item</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Applies to</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Solar Wi-Fi battery camera with panel</td><td>About $60–$300 each</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
<tr><td>4G LTE solar camera</td><td>About $150–$400 each</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
<tr><td>4G data plan</td><td>About $5–$30 per month</td><td>Solar (cellular)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cloud storage plan</td><td>About $3–$10 per camera per month, or multi-camera plans</td><td>Both (optional)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement battery or camera after 3–5 years</td><td>About $30–$150 per camera</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
<tr><td>4-camera PoE NVR kit</td><td>About $250–$700</td><td>Wired</td></tr>
<tr><td>8-camera PoE NVR kit</td><td>About $400–$1,200</td><td>Wired</td></tr>
<tr><td>Professional Ethernet run per camera (house)</td><td>About $100–$300 per drop</td><td>Wired</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trenching for buried cable</td><td>About $5–$15 per foot (more through rock, concrete or landscaping)</td><td>Wired, outbuildings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrician (new outdoor outlet or circuit)</td><td>About $150–$500 per outlet; hourly rates often $60–$130</td><td>Wired (plug-in)</td></tr>
<tr><td>UPS for NVR and PoE switch</td><td>About $80–$250</td><td>Wired (optional)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Here is how that plays out. Four cameras on a house with easy attic access might cost $400–$1,000 as a DIY PoE kit, roughly the same as four mid-range solar cameras. Add a camera on a gate 200 ft away, and the wired route needs about $1,000–$3,000 of trenching alone, while a solar or 4G solar camera covers it for a few hundred dollars. Over five years, a solar setup with cloud and cellular plans on every camera can overtake the wired cost, so run your own numbers.</p>
<!--tool:cost-->
<p>You can also open the full <a href="/tools/solar-vs-wired-cost-calculator/">solar vs wired cost calculator</a> to compare more scenarios, including subscriptions and battery replacement.</p>

<h2>Solar powered vs wired CCTV: reliability and video quality</h2>
<h3>Recording: 24/7 vs events</h3>
<p>This is the most important practical difference. A wired CCTV camera recording to an NVR captures everything, so you can scroll back to any moment. A solar camera captures clips that start when motion is detected and end after a set time. If the detector misses something, such as a person moving slowly straight toward the camera, there may be no footage at all. Large off-grid solar CCTV systems with 40–100+ W panels and big batteries can record continuously, but they are a different class of product from a consumer solar camera.</p>
<h3>Video quality</h3>
<p>Resolution labels can be the same, but solar cameras usually encode at lower bitrates and sometimes lower frame rates to save energy and upload time. Wired cameras can run at full bitrate around the clock and often have larger sensors and better low-light performance. For identifying faces or plates at distance, wired cameras typically have the edge. The <a href="/tools/camera-coverage-calculator/">camera coverage calculator</a> helps you check whether a given camera will deliver enough detail at your distances.</p>
<h3>Reliability over time</h3>
<p>A wired camera's reliability mostly depends on the cable and the NVR's hard drive. A solar camera depends on sun, battery health and signal, all of which vary. Owner reports commonly mention solar cameras running low in winter or after long cloudy spells, while wired cameras rarely need attention. On the other hand, solar cameras keep running during power cuts, whereas a wired system goes dark unless the NVR and switch are on a UPS. Our <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-in-winter/">winter guide</a> covers how to keep solar cameras charged in the dark months.</p>

<h2>How long do solar and wired cameras last?</h2>
<p>Lifespan is another quiet difference. A wired PoE camera has no battery, so with sensible mounting it can keep working for many years; the parts most likely to need replacing are the NVR's hard drive and, eventually, the cameras themselves as resolution standards move on. A solar camera's weak link is its lithium battery. Most lithium-ion cells lose noticeable capacity after roughly 300–500 full cycles, which in normal use is often two to four years, sooner in hot climates or very busy locations. Some solar cameras have replaceable batteries; many don't, which means replacing the whole camera when runtime drops.</p>
<p>Panels themselves last a long time, but small camera panels can yellow, crack or suffer water ingress at the connector. Budget for a battery or camera replacement in any five-year solar cost estimate.</p>

<h2>Decision matrix: solar or wired for your property?</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Recommendation</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Suburban home, easy attic access</td><td>Wired PoE at the house, solar for the far fence or shed</td><td>Cable runs are short and cheap; solar covers the awkward spots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suburban home, finished walls, no attic access</td><td>Solar or hybrid</td><td>Fishing cable through finished walls is costly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rental property</td><td>Solar (or plug-in battery cameras)</td><td>No drilling, takes cameras with you; check lease and local rules. See our <a href="/guides/rental-property-outdoor-camera-no-subscription/">rental camera guide</a></td></tr>
<tr><td>Farm or ranch</td><td>Solar, often 4G; wired only near the house</td><td>Long distances make trenching expensive</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small business or storefront</td><td>Wired PoE with NVR</td><td>Needs 24/7 footage, many cameras, tamper-resistant storage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Construction site</td><td>Solar CCTV tower or 4G solar cameras</td><td>Temporary, no power, must move as work progresses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vacation home or cabin</td><td>Solar 4G, or wired if internet and power are reliable</td><td>Power and internet may be off when unoccupied</td></tr>
<tr><td>High-traffic street-facing entrance</td><td>Wired</td><td>Hundreds of daily triggers would drain a battery</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For property-specific advice, see <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-for-farms/">solar cameras for farms</a>, <a href="/use-cases/solar-cameras-rural-property/">solar cameras for rural property</a> and <a href="/guides/remote-cabin-solar-camera/">remote cabin solar cameras</a>.</p>

<h2>Hybrid setups: combining PoE and solar</h2>
<p>You don't have to choose one technology for the whole property. A hybrid setup often gives the best results for the money.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PoE at the house, solar at the edges:</strong> wired cameras on entry doors and the driveway record 24/7 to an NVR, while solar cameras cover the gate, shed, garden and fence lines.</li>
<li><strong>Solar for outbuildings:</strong> a barn, workshop or garage far from the house gets a solar camera with Wi-Fi via a mesh node or a 4G SIM, avoiding trenching.</li>
<li><strong>Solar-powered PoE:</strong> for a remote spot that needs continuous recording, a larger off-grid solar kit (panel, charge controller, 12 V battery and a small PoE injector) can power a standard PoE camera. Size it carefully; continuous recording needs far more energy than a battery camera.</li>
<li><strong>One app where possible:</strong> some brands offer both PoE and battery cameras in one app, which keeps notifications in one place.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout tip"><strong>Rule of thumb</strong><p>If you can get a cable to the spot for less than the price of a solar camera, go wired. If the cable costs more than the camera, go solar.</p></div>

<h2>Which is right for you?</h2>
<p>Choose solar if your cameras need to go where power and cables don't reach, if you rent, or if you want a quick DIY install with no electrician. Choose wired PoE if you need continuous recording, run a business, cover a busy area or plan six or more cameras around one building. For most homeowners, the smart answer to solar vs wired security cameras is both: wired where it is easy, solar where it isn't.</p>
<p>Ready to pick hardware? See our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> picks and the <a href="/solar-camera-installation/">solar camera installation guide</a>, or read <a href="/guides/are-solar-cameras-worth-it/">are solar cameras worth it</a> for an honest look at the limitations.</p>
